health authorities across latin america  are battling a rise in coronavirus  infections  the region already has the highest  covert 19 death rate in the world  brazil has suffered the most fatalities  in the region with more than  half a million deaths that's followed by  mexico where  over 230 000 people have died and peru  has reported nearly 200  000 deaths dw's johann ramirez reports  from lima where the new  lambda variant is pushing the public  healthcare system to breaking point  in peru people are placing their hopes  for curbing the pandemic  on vaccination yet progress is slow  only some 15 percent of peruvians have  received at least one dose  one of the worst rates in latin america  but for health workers on the ground  it's better than nothing  we felt optimistic because people react  well to vaccination in peru  in fact younger people would like  everything to go faster so they can get  vaccinated soon although commerce has  reopened after a  nine month quarantine strict safety  measures remain in place  authorities have mandated using double  face masks to enter  stores and shopping centers face shields  are widely used and hand washing  facilities  had been installed in the streets but it  has not been enough  peru has the highest per capita death  rate in the world  the government's response to the crisis  has been widely  the criticized arrived too late  the management of the pandemic is a  complete disgrace  especially for the previous president's  one now at least the pfizer vaccine has  arrived  the last two peaks of infections in peru  have coincided  with deep political crisis that in both  cases  led to massive demonstrations the first  one was in november  when political instability led to three  presidents  in one week the second wave of protests  is happening right now the reason  a controversial presidential election  one of the candidates cried fraud while  the other  claimed there is a plan to steal his  victory supporters from both sides have  taken to the streets  according to some experts these  demonstrations could cause  a further rise in the infection curve  but at this point the country cannot  afford new lockdowns  the greatest effort must be done to  vaccinate  the population and to start negotiating  from now on  new generation vaccines for the new  variants that may appear  this is the most effective measure and  we have been slow in doing so  on average peru administers about 350  000 vaccines every week a low figure for  a country of more than  33 million authorities have promised  that the process will accelerate a  government forecast estimates  that the eligible population will be  immunized  by the end of 2021  cuba is expected to approve one of those  one of its vaccines against covert 19  in the coming days regulatory approval  for the shot called  abdallah could help speed up  vaccinations across latin america where  the case numbers  remain high but countries are struggling  to get shots  however public health officials are  calling for caution saying the vaccine  should be scrutinized by global drug  regulators before being used around the  world  a moment of celebration in havana last  month as cuban scientists announced the  results of their kova 19 vaccine trial  the shot's 92 reported efficacy rate  puts it in the same league as those  developed by pharma giants like the  biontech pfizer alliance  a remarkable achievement for a tiny  island of 11 million people  havana has five covet 19 vaccine  candidates in development  it's been vaccinating its own citizen  since may and last month started  shipping shots to venezuela  a point of national pride  we cubans help others things that other  countries in the world don't do  countries that have more possibilities  than we do and we help those in need  without any problem  cuba says 30 countries have expressed  interest in its vaccines  but public health officials and even  politicians are urging caution  cuba state biotech firm hasn't published  trial data yet or applied for approval  from the world health organization  if this vaccine is approved by the  international scientific community  we will welcome it but it must be  approved  we venezuelans should not be guinea pigs  but with no end in sight for latin  america's vaccine shortage  some are being forced to choose between  an unrecognized shot  or none at all
 

